 When a check fails: read the last lines of its output first; they name the file and line. For a test, run it alone (`python -m pytest -q tests/test_name.py::test_name`). A test that did not run is not a pass: to see why tests were skipped, run `python -m pytest -q -rs`, which prints one line per skip reason (for example "could not import 'playwright.sync_api'", "node is not installed", "no gitleaks program on PATH"). To make a missing secret scan a failure instead of a skip, set `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN=1` and `GITLEAKS_BIN` to the program's path.
 
+A known gap in the secret scan, measured on 2026-11-13 with gitleaks 8.30.1 (the version `lint.yml` installs) and this repository's `.gitleaks.toml`: gitleaks did not report a line whose value is 40 or more lowercase characters right after a `hadavin-dx_` prefix, for example a made-up second SonarQube project key in `sonar-project.properties` or after a name such as `x_api_key`, while the repository's own scan for hardcoded literals (`patterns.txt`, the pattern for 40 or more characters of letters, digits and `+/=_-`) did report both lines. So that pattern is the only check that catches this shape; a value shorter than 40 characters was not tested. The exception that lets the real project key through (the last line of `exceptions.txt` and the `sonar.projectKey` allowlist in `.gitleaks.toml`) therefore has to stay exact. `tests/test_gitleaks_exceptions.py` plants near-misses in a temporary copy and fails if either exception is widened. If you add a second key or secret of this shape, add its exact text, never a wider pattern or a whole file, and keep that test passing.
+
